window.TOPICS=window.TOPICS||{};window.TOPICS['cross-validation']={"id": "cross-validation", "title": "Cross-validation and model comparison", "tagline": "Choosing hyperparameters, k-fold and LOOCV, and which model can do what. 13 PYQs (42 marks) solved step by step.", "concepts": {"prereq": {"name": "Prerequisites (a line through points)", "marks": 0, "ease": 3, "repair": "<p>You only need to fit a line: slope from two points is rise over run, and for several points the least-squares slope is $S_{xy}/S_{xx}$ with centred sums. Each LOOCV question repeats this fit with one point removed.</p>"}, "tune": {"name": "Choosing hyperparameters from error tables", "marks": 6, "ease": 2, "repair": "<p>Training error always prefers the most flexible setting, so it cannot choose. Choose the setting with the lowest validation (or cross-validation) error. Then check: if training error is far below validation error, that setting is overfitting.</p>"}, "cvproc": {"name": "k-fold cross-validation: counts", "marks": 3, "ease": 1, "repair": "<p>Split the data into $k$ equal folds. Train $k$ times, each time holding out a different fold for validation. So: $k$ models per setting, each trained on $n(k-1)/k$ points and validated on $n/k$. Leave-one-out is the case $k=n$.</p>"}, "loolr": {"name": "LOOCV for a line", "marks": 12, "ease": 3, "repair": "<p>For each point in turn: remove it, fit the line on the others, predict the removed point, and square the error. Average the squared errors. Number the points in table order so you remove the one the question names.</p>"}, "zero": {"name": "Zero training error", "marks": 6, "ease": 2, "repair": "<p>Zero training error needs a model that can memorise: a look-up table, a fully grown tree, or 1-NN. Linear models, Naive Bayes and k-NN with larger $k$ can be forced into mistakes by awkward labels.</p>"}, "compare": {"name": "Model comparison: true/false facts and geometry", "marks": 15, "ease": 3, "repair": "<p>Match each model to its boundary shape: trees cut along the axes (staircase), linear models and logistic regression draw straight lines, k-NN and Gaussian Naive Bayes can bend. Use that picture to decide true/false statements quickly.</p>"}}, "sections": [{"id": "orient", "title": "Orientation: what you will learn and why", "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">A model that fits its own training data perfectly may still fail on new data. To <b>choose</b> settings (like $\\lambda$, $k$ or tree depth) honestly we must test on data the model did not train on. <b>Cross-validation</b> does this by repeatedly holding out part of the data. This topic also collects the \"which model can do what\" questions that appear in many papers.</p>\n<h3>What you will be able to do</h3>\n<ul><li>Explain training, validation and test sets and recognise overfitting and underfitting from an error table.</li><li>Choose a hyperparameter from a table of training and validation errors.</li><li>Count how many models $k$-fold cross-validation trains and how big each training set is.</li><li>Run leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV) for a line $y=w_0+w_1x$ by hand.</li><li>Decide which models can certainly reach zero training error.</li><li>Answer true/false questions that compare decision trees, k-NN, Naive Bayes and regression models.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why this topic matters for Quiz 2</h3>\n<p>Counted only from the PYQs in this project (not a prediction):</p>\n<table><tr><th>What the PYQs ask</th><th>PYQs</th><th>Marks</th></tr><tr><td>Choosing hyperparameters from error tables</td><td>2</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>k-fold cross-validation: counts</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>LOOCV for a line</td><td>3</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>Zero training error</td><td>2</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Model comparison: true/false facts and geometry</td><td>4</td><td>15</td></tr></table>\n<p>In total <b>13 PYQs worth 42 marks</b> across <b>6 papers</b> (5 MCQ, 4 MSQ, 4 numeric).</p>\n<h3>How this topic connects</h3>\n<p>It uses the line fit from the Linear regression topic, ridge from the Ridge topic, and k-NN and decision trees from their topics. The k-NN topic already showed LOOCV with a vote; here the model is a line.</p>\n<div class=\"notice\">Tags: <span class=\"tag SRC\">[SRC]</span> from your files, <span class=\"tag DERIVED\">[DERIVED]</span> answer I worked out because the bank gives no key, <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span> my own example, <span class=\"tag FLAG\">[FLAG]</span> disagreement between my calculation and a key.</div>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><div class=\"bt\">How to use this page</div><p>Read each section, do the quick checks, then attempt each PYQ before opening its solution.</p></div><h3>What is new in this upgrade <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3><ul><li>A <b>big picture</b> section (next): concept map, train/validation/test table, k-fold against leave-one-out, counting table, mnemonics.</li><li><b>Figures</b>: the Aug 2025 table, five folds, and a four-point leave-one-out.</li><li>Recall questions, a PYQ pattern map, variations and a flowchart. All new material is tagged <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span>.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "overview", "title": "Big picture: concept map, comparison table and the recipe", "html": "\n<p><span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span> Read this page first. It shows why we validate, how folds work and which facts the exam asks.</p>\n<h3>The concept map</h3>\n<figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 680 300\" style=\"width:680px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Concept map of cross-validation: split, fit, score, choose the lowest validation error\"><defs><marker id=\"cc\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"10\" y=\"20\" width=\"150\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.4\"/><text x=\"85.0\" y=\"42.0\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-weight:700;\">Candidate settings</text><text x=\"85.0\" y=\"60.0\" font-size=\"10.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">lambda, k, depth, ...</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"20\" width=\"150\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--ex-bg);stroke:var(--ex-bd);stroke-width:1.4\"/><text x=\"275.0\" y=\"42.0\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-weight:700;\">Split the data</text><text x=\"275.0\" y=\"60.0\" font-size=\"10.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">train | validation</text><rect x=\"390\" y=\"20\" width=\"130\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--ex-bg);stroke:var(--ex-bd);stroke-width:1.4\"/><text x=\"455.0\" y=\"42.0\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-weight:700;\">Fit + score</text><text x=\"455.0\" y=\"60.0\" font-size=\"10.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">validation error</text><rect x=\"560\" y=\"20\" width=\"110\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bg);stroke:var(--bad-bd);stroke-width:1.4\"/><text x=\"615.0\" y=\"42.0\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-weight:700;\">Pick lowest</text><text x=\"615.0\" y=\"60.0\" font-size=\"10.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">validation error</text><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"198\" y2=\"48\" marker-end=\"url(#cc)\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);stroke-width:1.6;\"/><line x1=\"350\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"388\" y2=\"48\" marker-end=\"url(#cc)\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);stroke-width:1.6;\"/><line x1=\"520\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"558\" y2=\"48\" marker-end=\"url(#cc)\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);stroke-width:1.6;\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"130\" width=\"300\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--ex-bg);stroke:var(--ex-bd);stroke-width:1.4\"/><text x=\"160.0\" y=\"152.0\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-weight:700;\">k-fold</text><text x=\"160.0\" y=\"170.0\" font-size=\"10.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">k models, train on n(k-1)/k, validate on n/k</text><rect x=\"370\" y=\"130\" width=\"300\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--ex-bg);stroke:var(--ex-bd);stroke-width:1.4\"/><text x=\"520.0\" y=\"152.0\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-weight:700;\">Leave-one-out (k = n)</text><text x=\"520.0\" y=\"170.0\" font-size=\"10.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">n models, train on n-1, validate on 1</text><path d=\"M285 76 L285 104 L160 104 L160 128\" marker-end=\"url(#cc)\" style=\"fill:none;stroke:var(--muted);stroke-width:1.6\"/><path d=\"M285 104 L520 104 L520 128\" marker-end=\"url(#cc)\" style=\"fill:none;stroke:var(--muted);stroke-width:1.6\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"220\" width=\"660\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.4\"/><text x=\"340.0\" y=\"244.0\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-weight:700;\">The test set is never used for choosing: it is kept for one final honest score.</text><text x=\"340.0\" y=\"264.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">Training error alone always favours the most flexible model, so it cannot choose the setting.</text></svg><figcaption>Try each candidate setting, score it on data it was not trained on, pick the lowest validation error. k-fold and leave-one-out differ only in how the data is split.</figcaption></figure>\n<h3>Train, validation, test: three jobs <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3>\n<table><tr><th>Set</th><th>Used to</th><th>Used for choosing?</th><th>Used once?</th></tr><tr><td>Training</td><td>fit the weights / build the tree</td><td>no</td><td>many times</td></tr><tr><td>Validation</td><td>compare settings</td><td><b>yes</b></td><td>many times</td></tr><tr><td>Test</td><td>report the final score</td><td>no</td><td><b>once</b></td></tr></table>\n<h3>k-fold against leave-one-out <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>k-fold ($k&lt;n$)</th><th>Leave-one-out ($k=n$)</th></tr><tr><td>Models trained</td><td>$k$</td><td>$n$</td></tr><tr><td>Training size</td><td>$n\\frac{k-1}{k}$</td><td>$n-1$</td></tr><tr><td>Validation size</td><td>$n/k$</td><td>$1$</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>smaller</td><td>largest (n fits)</td></tr><tr><td>Estimate</td><td>a little pessimistic (less training data)</td><td>nearly unbiased, can vary more</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Counting, with examples <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3>\n<table><tr><th>$n$</th><th>$k$</th><th>Models (one setting)</th><th>Training size</th><th>Validation size</th></tr><tr><td>100</td><td>5</td><td>5</td><td>80</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>1000</td><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>750</td><td>250</td></tr><tr><td>50</td><td>50</td><td>50</td><td>49</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>600</td><td>10</td><td>10</td><td>540</td><td>60</td></tr></table>\n<p>With $5$ candidate values of a hyperparameter and $k=4$, a full search trains $5\\times4=20$ models. Read whether the question says \"one value\" or \"all values\".</p>\n<h3>Mnemonics and recognition cues <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3>\n<ul><li><b>\"Validate to choose, test to report.\"</b></li><li><b>\"k models, not k times the data.\"</b> Each model sees $n(k-1)/k$ points.</li><li><b>\"Leave it out, then fit.\"</b> The left-out point must not touch the fit.</li><li><b>\"Low train, high validation = overfit; both high = underfit.\"</b></li><li><b>Cue \"each data point is its own neighbour\":</b> $1$-NN has zero training error (distinct points).</li></ul>\n<h3>Analogy and its limit <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3>\n<table><tr><th>Analogy</th><th>Good for</th><th>Where it breaks</th></tr><tr><td>Practice exams: study from some, test yourself on the one you held back</td><td>why the held-out part must stay unseen</td><td>every fold is eventually used for training, so cross-validation scores are not a final test; keep a separate test set</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Recall questions</h3>\n<details class=\"rc\"><summary>$n=100$, $k=5$: models and training size?</summary><p>$5$ models, $80$ points each.</p></details>\n<details class=\"rc\"><summary>Leave-one-out on $n=50$: how many models?</summary><p>$50$, each trained on $49$ points.</p></details>\n"}, {"id": "prereq", "title": "Start here: a line through points", "html": "\n<h3>Piece 1. Fitting $y=w_0+w_1x$</h3>\n<div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">Formula: simple linear regression</div><p>$$w_1=\\frac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}},\\qquad w_0=\\bar y-w_1\\bar x,\\qquad S_{xx}=\\sum(x_i-\\bar x)^2,\\ S_{xy}=\\sum(x_i-\\bar x)(y_i-\\bar y)$$ $\\bar x$ and $\\bar y$ are the means of the $x$ and $y$ values; $w_1$ is the slope and $w_0$ the intercept of the least-squares line.</p></div>\n<div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example: Simple: two points</div>\n<p><b>Problem.</b> Points $(1,2)$ and $(0,1)$. Find $w_0,w_1$.</p><p><b>What is asked.</b> The line.</p><p><b>Idea / formula used.</b> Two points define a line exactly.</p>\n<div class=\"wk-step\">Slope $=(2-1)/(1-0)=1$.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">Intercept: at $x=0$, $y=1$, so $w_0=1$.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">So $y=1+1\\cdot x$ and $\\|w\\|^2=1^2+1^2=2$.</div>\n<div class=\"wk-after\"><div class=\"final\">Answer: $w=(1,1)$</div><div class=\"mistake\">Common mistake: using $\\sum xy/\\sum x^2$ without the intercept.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example: Exam level: three points</div>\n<p><b>Problem.</b> Points $(3,8),(0,3),(6,13)$. Find the least-squares line.</p><p><b>What is asked.</b> $w_0,w_1$.</p><p><b>Idea / formula used.</b> Means, then $S_{xx}$ and $S_{xy}$.</p>\n<div class=\"wk-step\">$\\bar x=(3+0+6)/3=3$, $\\bar y=(8+3+13)/3=8$.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">$S_{xx}=(0)^2+(-3)^2+(3)^2=18$.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">$S_{xy}=(0)(0)+(-3)(-5)+(3)(5)=30$.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">$w_1=30/18=1.6667$, $w_0=8-1.6667\\cdot3=3$.</div>\n<div class=\"wk-after\"><div class=\"final\">Answer: $y=3+1.667x$</div><div class=\"mistake\">Common mistake: forgetting to subtract the means.</div></div></div>\n<h3>Piece 2. Three kinds of data</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Set</th><th>Used for</th><th>Used to choose settings?</th></tr><tr><td>Training</td><td>fit the weights</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td>Validation</td><td>compare settings (hyperparameters)</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>Test</td><td>final honest score</td><td>never</td></tr></table>", "quiz": [{"id": "pq1", "concept": "prereq", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "Line through $(0,2)$ and $(1,5)$. Slope?", "num": {"ans": 3, "tol": 0.001, "show": "3"}, "explain": "$(5-2)/(1-0)=3$.", "hint": "Rise over run."}, {"id": "pq2", "concept": "prereq", "level": "moderate", "type": "num", "stem": "Points $(0,1),(2,5)$: intercept $w_0$ of the line?", "num": {"ans": 1, "tol": 0.001, "show": "1"}, "explain": "At $x=0$, $y=1$.", "hint": "Read at x=0."}, {"id": "pq3", "concept": "prereq", "level": "moderate", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Which set is used to compare hyperparameter settings?", "options": [{"t": "validation set", "ok": true, "why": "Not used for fitting weights."}, {"t": "test set", "ok": false, "why": "Keep it untouched."}, {"t": "training set only", "ok": false, "why": "It favours overfitting."}, {"t": "none", "ok": false, "why": "We need one."}], "explain": "Validation.", "hint": "Not train, not test."}]}, {"id": "tune", "title": "Reading training and validation errors; choosing a hyperparameter", "html": "\n<h3>Intuition</h3>\n<div class=\"box analogy\"><div class=\"bt\">Everyday analogy</div><p>A student who memorises last year's paper scores full marks on it (training) but poorly on a new paper (validation). The honest score is the new paper's, so that is what we use to choose between students (settings).</p></div>\n<table><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Training error</th><th>Validation error</th><th>Diagnosis</th></tr><tr><td>gap is big</td><td>very low</td><td>much higher</td><td>overfitting (too flexible)</td></tr><tr><td>both high</td><td>high</td><td>high</td><td>underfitting (too rigid)</td></tr><tr><td>both low and close</td><td>low</td><td>low</td><td>good fit</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">Formula: Selection rule</div><p>Choose the setting with the <b>lowest validation error</b>. Training error alone is misleading because it keeps falling as the model becomes more flexible.</p></div>\n<h3>Worked examples</h3>\n<div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example: Exam level: Aug 2025 table</div>\n<p><b>Problem.</b> Settings $(\\text{lr},\\text{iterations})$: $1:(0.05,1000)$ train $150$ val $160$; $2:(0.10,800)$ train $145$ val $150$; $3:(0.15,500)$ train $140$ val $190$; $4:(0.25,350)$ train $170$ val $180$. Which setting?</p><p><b>What is asked.</b> The best setting and why.</p><p><b>Idea / formula used.</b> Lowest validation error, but also check the gap.</p>\n<div class=\"wk-step\">Validation errors: $160,150,190,180$. Lowest: setting $2$ ($150$).</div><div class=\"wk-step\">Setting 3 has the lowest training error ($140$) but validation $190$: a gap of $50$, a sign of overfitting.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">Setting 4 has both errors high ($170$, $180$): underfitting.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">Setting 2 has low validation error and a small gap ($5$).</div>\n<div class=\"wk-after\"><div class=\"final\">Answer: Setting 2</div><div class=\"mistake\">Common mistake: choosing the lowest training error.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example: Exam level: ridge $\\lambda$ table</div>\n<p><b>Problem.</b> $\\lambda=0.001$: train $1.25$, val $8.40$; $0.1$: $2.10$, $4.20$; $1.0$: $3.45$, $3.15$; $10$: $5.80$, $3.20$; $100$: $12.40$, $11.90$. Which $\\lambda$?</p><p><b>What is asked.</b> The best $\\lambda$.</p><p><b>Idea / formula used.</b> Lowest validation error.</p>\n<div class=\"wk-step\">Validation errors: $8.40,\\ 4.20,\\ 3.15,\\ 3.20,\\ 11.90$.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">Lowest is $3.15$ at $\\lambda=1.0$.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">$\\lambda=10$ is very close ($3.20$) but not lower.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">Small $\\lambda$ overfits (big gap), $\\lambda=100$ underfits (both errors large).</div>\n<div class=\"wk-after\"><div class=\"final\">Answer: $\\lambda=1.0$</div><div class=\"mistake\">Common mistake: picking $\\lambda=0.001$ because its training error is smallest.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Trap: training error is not the criterion</div><p>Never pick a setting just because its training error is lowest. The validation error decides.</p></div>\n<h3>The Aug 2025 table as a picture <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3>\n<figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 560 300\" style=\"width:560px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Training and validation error for the four settings of the Aug 2025 question\"><line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"230.0\" x2=\"530.0\" y2=\"230.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\"/><line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"40.0\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"230.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\"/><line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"206.0\" x2=\"530.0\" y2=\"206.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--line);fill:none\"/><text x=\"54.0\" y=\"210.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">140</text><line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"158.0\" x2=\"530.0\" y2=\"158.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--line);fill:none\"/><text x=\"54.0\" y=\"162.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">160</text><line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"110.0\" x2=\"530.0\" y2=\"110.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--line);fill:none\"/><text x=\"54.0\" y=\"114.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">180</text><line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"62.0\" x2=\"530.0\" y2=\"62.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--line);fill:none\"/><text x=\"54.0\" y=\"66.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">200</text><path d=\"M120.0 182.0 L230.0 194.0 L340.0 206.0 L450.0 134.0\" style=\"fill:none;stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:2.4\"/><path d=\"M120.0 158.0 L230.0 182.0 L340.0 86.0 L450.0 110.0\" style=\"fill:none;stroke:var(--bad-bd);stroke-width:2.4;stroke-dasharray:6 4\"/><circle cx=\"120.0\" cy=\"182.0\" r=\"4.5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:1.2\"/><text x=\"120.0\" y=\"198.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--accent);\">150</text><circle cx=\"230.0\" cy=\"194.0\" r=\"4.5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:1.2\"/><text x=\"230.0\" y=\"210.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--accent);\">145</text><circle cx=\"340.0\" cy=\"206.0\" r=\"4.5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:1.2\"/><text x=\"340.0\" y=\"222.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--accent);\">140</text><circle cx=\"450.0\" cy=\"134.0\" r=\"4.5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:1.2\"/><text x=\"450.0\" y=\"150.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--accent);\">170</text><circle cx=\"120.0\" cy=\"158.0\" r=\"4.5\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bg);stroke:var(--bad-bd);stroke-width:1.2\"/><text x=\"120.0\" y=\"149.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bd);\">160</text><circle cx=\"230.0\" cy=\"182.0\" r=\"4.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ex-bg);stroke:var(--ex-bd);stroke-width:1.2\"/><text x=\"230.0\" y=\"173.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bd);\">150</text><circle cx=\"340.0\" cy=\"86.0\" r=\"4.5\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bg);stroke:var(--bad-bd);stroke-width:1.2\"/><text x=\"340.0\" y=\"77.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bd);\">190</text><circle cx=\"450.0\" cy=\"110.0\" r=\"4.5\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bg);stroke:var(--bad-bd);stroke-width:1.2\"/><text x=\"450.0\" y=\"101.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bd);\">180</text><text x=\"120.0\" y=\"246.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">setting 1</text><text x=\"230.0\" y=\"246.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">setting 2</text><text x=\"340.0\" y=\"246.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">setting 3</text><text x=\"450.0\" y=\"246.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">setting 4</text><text x=\"80.0\" y=\"282.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">solid blue: training   dashed red: validation   green dot: chosen (lowest validation)</text></svg><figcaption>Real numbers from the Aug 2025 question. Setting 3 has the lowest training error (140) but the worst validation error (190); setting 2 has the lowest validation error (150).</figcaption></figure>\n<h3>Diagnosis table <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3>\n<table><tr><th>Sign</th><th>Reading</th><th>What to try</th></tr><tr><td>train $\\ll$ validation</td><td>overfitting</td><td>more regularisation, simpler model, fewer iterations</td></tr><tr><td>both high</td><td>underfitting</td><td>less regularisation, more flexible model</td></tr><tr><td>both low, close</td><td>good fit</td><td>keep</td></tr><tr><td>validation rises while training falls</td><td>overfitting has begun</td><td>stop earlier / pick the earlier setting</td></tr></table>\n<details class=\"rc\"><summary>Setting A: train 5, validation 30. Setting B: train 12, validation 14. Which?</summary><p>B (lowest validation).</p></details>\n<details class=\"rc\"><summary>Can training error pick the best $\\lambda$?</summary><p>No, it keeps falling as flexibility grows.</p></details>\n", "quiz": [{"id": "u1", "concept": "tune", "level": "easy", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Setting A: train $2$, val $9$. Setting B: train $5$, val $6$. Which is better?", "options": [{"t": "B", "ok": true, "why": "Lower validation error."}, {"t": "A", "ok": false, "why": "Overfits."}, {"t": "Equal", "ok": false, "why": "Validation differs."}, {"t": "Cannot say", "ok": false, "why": "We can."}], "explain": "Lowest validation.", "hint": "Compare 9 and 6."}, {"id": "u2", "concept": "tune", "level": "moderate", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Training error very low, validation error much higher. This is...", "options": [{"t": "overfitting", "ok": true, "why": "Memorised the training data."}, {"t": "underfitting", "ok": false, "why": "That is both high."}, {"t": "a good fit", "ok": false, "why": "Gap is large."}, {"t": "noise-free data", "ok": false, "why": "Not implied."}], "explain": "Gap means overfit.", "hint": "Look at the gap."}, {"id": "u3", "concept": "tune", "level": "moderate", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Both training and validation errors are high. This is...", "options": [{"t": "underfitting", "ok": true, "why": "Model too simple."}, {"t": "overfitting", "ok": false, "why": "That has a big gap."}, {"t": "perfect", "ok": false, "why": "Errors are high."}, {"t": "impossible", "ok": false, "why": "Happens with $\\lambda$ too large."}], "explain": "Both high.", "hint": "No gap."}]}, {"id": "cvproc", "title": "How k-fold cross-validation works: counting models and sizes", "html": "\n<h3>Intuition</h3>\n<div class=\"box analogy\"><div class=\"bt\">Everyday analogy</div><p>Split a class into $k$ equal study groups. Let each group take its turn as the \"exam group\" while the other $k-1$ groups are used to study. Everyone gets examined exactly once.</p></div>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"bt\">Definition: k-fold cross-validation</div><p>Split the $n$ points into $k$ equal parts (folds). For each fold $j=1,\\dots,k$: train on the other $k-1$ folds, evaluate on fold $j$. Average the $k$ validation errors.</p></div>\n<div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">Formula: Counts</div><p>$$\\text{models trained (for one setting)}=k,\\qquad \\text{training size}=n\\cdot\\frac{k-1}{k},\\qquad \\text{validation size}=\\frac nk$$ $n$ is the number of data points and $k$ the number of folds. Leave-one-out is the special case $k=n$: $n$ models, each trained on $n-1$ points.</p></div>\n<p>If you test several values of a hyperparameter (say $5$ values of $\\lambda$), the total number of models is $5\\times k$. The question wording says whether it asks for \"one value of $\\lambda$\" or for all.</p>\n<div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example: Simple: counts</div>\n<p><b>Problem.</b> $n=1000$, $k=4$ folds, one value of $\\lambda$. How many models are trained and how big is each training set?</p><p><b>What is asked.</b> Both numbers.</p><p><b>Idea / formula used.</b> One model per fold.</p>\n<div class=\"wk-step\">$k=4$ folds, so $4$ models.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">Each fold has $1000/4=250$ points and is held out once.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">Training set $=1000-250=750$ points.</div>\n<div class=\"wk-after\"><div class=\"final\">Answer: $4$ models, $750$ training points each.</div><div class=\"mistake\">Common mistake: answering $250$ (that is the validation size) or $1000$.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example: Tricky: leave-one-out counts</div>\n<p><b>Problem.</b> $n=50$ points, leave-one-out. Models and training size?</p><p><b>What is asked.</b> Both numbers.</p><p><b>Idea / formula used.</b> $k=n$.</p>\n<div class=\"wk-step\">$k=n=50$, so $50$ models.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">Each trains on $50-1=49$ points.</div>\n<div class=\"wk-after\"><div class=\"final\">Answer: $50$ models of $49$ points</div><div class=\"mistake\">Common mistake: saying $1$ model.</div></div></div>\n<h3>Five folds, drawn <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3>\n<figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 620 270\" style=\"width:620px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Five-fold cross-validation: each fold is the validation set once and the training set four times\"><text x=\"315.0\" y=\"24.0\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-weight:700;\">data split into k = 5 equal folds (columns)</text><text x=\"80.0\" y=\"74.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">round 1</text><rect x=\"90\" y=\"54\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bg);stroke:var(--bad-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"132.0\" y=\"74.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bd);font-weight:700;\">validate</text><rect x=\"180\" y=\"54\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"222.0\" y=\"74.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"270\" y=\"54\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"312.0\" y=\"74.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"360\" y=\"54\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"402.0\" y=\"74.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"450\" y=\"54\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"492.0\" y=\"74.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><text x=\"80.0\" y=\"110.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">round 2</text><rect x=\"90\" y=\"90\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"132.0\" y=\"110.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"180\" y=\"90\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bg);stroke:var(--bad-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"222.0\" y=\"110.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bd);font-weight:700;\">validate</text><rect x=\"270\" y=\"90\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"312.0\" y=\"110.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"360\" y=\"90\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"402.0\" y=\"110.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"450\" y=\"90\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"492.0\" y=\"110.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><text x=\"80.0\" y=\"146.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">round 3</text><rect x=\"90\" y=\"126\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"132.0\" y=\"146.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"180\" y=\"126\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"222.0\" y=\"146.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"270\" y=\"126\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bg);stroke:var(--bad-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"312.0\" y=\"146.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bd);font-weight:700;\">validate</text><rect x=\"360\" y=\"126\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"402.0\" y=\"146.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"450\" y=\"126\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"492.0\" y=\"146.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><text x=\"80.0\" y=\"182.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">round 4</text><rect x=\"90\" y=\"162\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"132.0\" y=\"182.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"180\" y=\"162\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"222.0\" y=\"182.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"270\" y=\"162\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"312.0\" y=\"182.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"360\" y=\"162\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bg);stroke:var(--bad-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"402.0\" y=\"182.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bd);font-weight:700;\">validate</text><rect x=\"450\" y=\"162\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"492.0\" y=\"182.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><text x=\"80.0\" y=\"218.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">round 5</text><rect x=\"90\" y=\"198\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"132.0\" y=\"218.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"180\" y=\"198\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"222.0\" y=\"218.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"270\" y=\"198\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"312.0\" y=\"218.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"360\" y=\"198\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"402.0\" y=\"218.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">train</text><rect x=\"450\" y=\"198\" width=\"84\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bg);stroke:var(--bad-bd);stroke-width:1.3\"/><text x=\"492.0\" y=\"218.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bd);font-weight:700;\">validate</text><text x=\"315.0\" y=\"260.0\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">Each fold validates once; the 5 validation errors are averaged. 5 models, each trained on 4/5 of the data.</text></svg><figcaption>Each column is one fold. In round r the r-th fold is held out for validation and the other four are used for training.</figcaption></figure>\n<h3>Counting slips <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3>\n<table><tr><th>Slip</th><th>Correct</th></tr><tr><td>training size $=n/k$</td><td>$n(k-1)/k$ (that is the validation size plus the rest)</td></tr><tr><td>models $=n$ for $k$-fold</td><td>$k$ models</td></tr><tr><td>forgetting the number of settings</td><td>total $=\\text{settings}\\times k$</td></tr></table>\n<details class=\"rc\"><summary>$n=600$, $k=10$: validation size?</summary><p>$60$.</p></details>\n<details class=\"rc\"><summary>Which $k$ gives leave-one-out?</summary><p>$k=n$.</p></details>\n<div class=\"explore\"><div class=\"bt\">Interactive explorer</div><p>Each row is one training run. Yellow points are held out for validation in that run, blue points train the model. Set $k=n$ to see leave-one-out.</p><div class=\"q1 q1w\"><div data-widget=\"folds\" data-n=\"12\" data-k=\"4\"></div></div></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "c1", "concept": "cvproc", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "$n=600$, $k=3$ folds. Training size per model?", "num": {"ans": 400, "tol": 0.001, "show": "400"}, "explain": "$600\\cdot2/3$.", "hint": "k-1 folds."}, {"id": "c2", "concept": "cvproc", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "5-fold CV, one value of $\\lambda$. Models trained?", "num": {"ans": 5, "tol": 0.001, "show": "5"}, "explain": "One per fold.", "hint": "k."}, {"id": "c3", "concept": "cvproc", "level": "moderate", "type": "num", "stem": "10-fold CV with $4$ candidate values of $\\lambda$. Total models?", "num": {"ans": 40, "tol": 0.001, "show": "40"}, "explain": "$4\\times10$.", "hint": "Multiply."}, {"id": "c4", "concept": "cvproc", "level": "moderate", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Leave-one-out with $n$ points trains...", "options": [{"t": "$n$ models, each on $n-1$ points", "ok": true, "why": "$k=n$."}, {"t": "$1$ model on $n$ points", "ok": false, "why": "That is plain training."}, {"t": "$n$ models on $n$ points", "ok": false, "why": "One point is held out."}, {"t": "$n-1$ models", "ok": false, "why": "One per point."}], "explain": "$k=n$.", "hint": "Hold out one at a time."}]}, {"id": "loolr", "title": "Leave-one-out cross-validation for a line", "html": "\n<h3>Procedure</h3>\n<ol><li>Take the left-out point; remove it.</li><li>Fit the line $y=w_0+w_1x$ on the remaining points.</li><li>Predict the left-out point: $\\hat y=w_0+w_1x_{out}$.</li><li>Compare with the true $y_{out}$ (squared error $(\\hat y-y_{out})^2$).</li><li>Repeat for each point and average the errors.</li></ol>\n<h3>Worked examples</h3>\n<div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example: Simple: weight when one point is left out</div>\n<p><b>Problem.</b> $D=\\{(1,2),(2,2),(0,1)\\}$, model $y=w_0+w_1x$. The point $(2,2)$ is the validation point. Find $\\|w\\|_2^2$.</p><p><b>What is asked.</b> The squared norm of the weights.</p><p><b>Idea / formula used.</b> Fit on the other two points.</p>\n<div class=\"wk-step\">Training points: $(1,2)$ and $(0,1)$.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">Slope $=1$, intercept $=1$ (see the first section).</div><div class=\"wk-step\">$\\|w\\|_2^2=1^2+1^2=2$.</div>\n<div class=\"wk-after\"><div class=\"final\">Answer: $2$</div><div class=\"mistake\">Common mistake: fitting on all three points or including the validation point.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example: Exam level: full LOOCV on four points</div>\n<p><b>Problem.</b> $(3,8),(0,3),(5,12),(6,13)$. Run LOOCV.</p><p><b>What is asked.</b> Fitted line and prediction for each left-out point.</p><p><b>Idea / formula used.</b> Refit four times.</p>\n<div class=\"wk-step\">Leave out $(3,8)$: fit on the other three gives $w_0=3.065$, $w_1=1.710$; prediction $8.194$ against true 8.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">Leave out $(0,3)$: fit on the other three gives $w_0=3.000$, $w_1=1.714$; prediction $3.000$ against true 3.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">Leave out $(5,12)$: fit on the other three gives $w_0=3.000$, $w_1=1.667$; prediction $11.333$ against true 12.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">Leave out $(6,13)$: fit on the other three gives $w_0=2.895$, $w_1=1.789$; prediction $13.632$ against true 13.</div><div class=\"wk-step\">Squared errors: 0.037, 0.000, 0.444, 0.399; their mean (the LOOCV mean squared error) is $0.220$.</div>\n<div class=\"wk-after\"><div class=\"final\">Answer: The LOOCV MSE is about $0.220$.</div><div class=\"mistake\">Common mistake: using the same fitted line for all four predictions.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Trap: which point is left out</div><p>When a question says \"the 3rd sample is the validation point\", number from $1$ in the table order: here sample 3 is $(5,12)$.</p></div>\n<h3>The four-point LOOCV, drawn <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3>\n<figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 300\" style=\"width:600px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Four points and the four lines fitted in leave-one-out cross-validation\"><line x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"250.0\" x2=\"570.0\" y2=\"250.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\"/><line x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"250.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"30.0\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);fill:none\"/><text x=\"70.0\" y=\"266.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">0</text><text x=\"140.0\" y=\"266.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">1</text><text x=\"210.0\" y=\"266.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">2</text><text x=\"280.0\" y=\"266.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">3</text><text x=\"350.0\" y=\"266.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">4</text><text x=\"420.0\" y=\"266.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">5</text><text x=\"490.0\" y=\"266.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">6</text><text x=\"560.0\" y=\"266.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">7</text><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"254.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">0</text><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"179.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">5</text><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"104.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">10</text><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"29.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">15</text><path d=\"M70.0 204.0 L560.0 24.5\" style=\"fill:none;stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:1.6;opacity:.75\"/><path d=\"M70.0 205.0 L560.0 25.0\" style=\"fill:none;stroke:var(--bad-bd);stroke-width:1.6;opacity:.75\"/><path d=\"M70.0 205.0 L560.0 30.0\" style=\"fill:none;stroke:var(--ex-bd);stroke-width:1.6;opacity:.75\"/><path d=\"M70.0 206.6 L560.0 18.7\" style=\"fill:none;stroke:var(--muted);stroke-width:1.6;opacity:.75\"/><circle cx=\"280.0\" cy=\"130.0\" r=\"5.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ex-bg);stroke:var(--accent);stroke-width:1.2\"/><text x=\"290.0\" y=\"148.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);paint-order:stroke;stroke:var(--surface);stroke-width:4px;stroke-linejoin:round\">(3, 8)</text><circle cx=\"70.0\" cy=\"205.0\" r=\"5.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ex-bg);stroke:var(--bad-bd);stroke-width:1.2\"/><text x=\"80.0\" y=\"223.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);paint-order:stroke;stroke:var(--surface);stroke-width:4px;stroke-linejoin:round\">(0, 3)</text><circle cx=\"420.0\" cy=\"70.0\" r=\"5.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ex-bg);stroke:var(--ex-bd);stroke-width:1.2\"/><text x=\"430.0\" y=\"88.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);paint-order:stroke;stroke:var(--surface);stroke-width:4px;stroke-linejoin:round\">(5, 12)</text><circle cx=\"490.0\" cy=\"55.0\" r=\"5.5\" style=\"fill:var(--ex-bg);stroke:var(--muted);stroke-width:1.2\"/><text x=\"500.0\" y=\"73.0\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"start\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);paint-order:stroke;stroke:var(--surface);stroke-width:4px;stroke-linejoin:round\">(6, 13)</text><text x=\"330.0\" y=\"288.0\" font-size=\"10.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">Each line is fitted with one point left out (same colour as that point). Predictions: 8.19, 3.00, 11.33, 13.63</text></svg><figcaption>Each line leaves out one point (same colour). The lines barely move, because the points are nearly collinear; the predictions 8.19, 3.00, 11.33, 13.63 are close to the true values $8,3,12,13$.</figcaption></figure>\n<table><tr><th>Left out</th><th>Prediction</th><th>True</th><th>Squared error</th></tr><tr><td>(3, 8)</td><td>8.194</td><td>8</td><td>0.037</td></tr><tr><td>(0, 3)</td><td>3.000</td><td>3</td><td>0.000</td></tr><tr><td>(5, 12)</td><td>11.333</td><td>12</td><td>0.444</td></tr><tr><td>(6, 13)</td><td>13.632</td><td>13</td><td>0.399</td></tr></table>\n<p>Mean of the four squared errors: 0.220. A large error flags a point that the rest of the data cannot predict (here $(5,12)$).</p>\n<details class=\"rc\"><summary>In LOOCV, is the left-out point used to fit its own line?</summary><p>No, never.</p></details>\n", "quiz": [{"id": "l1", "concept": "loolr", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "Points $(0,0),(2,4)$ train a line. Prediction at $x=1$?", "num": {"ans": 2, "tol": 0.001, "show": "2"}, "explain": "Slope $2$, intercept $0$: $y=2$ at $x=1$.", "hint": "Fit exactly."}, {"id": "l2", "concept": "loolr", "level": "moderate", "type": "num", "stem": "Train points $(0,1),(1,3)$. Squared error of predicting a left-out point $(2,6)$?", "num": {"ans": 1, "tol": 0.001, "show": "1"}, "explain": "Line $y=1+2x$ gives $5$ at $x=2$; $(5-6)^2=1$.", "hint": "Fit, predict, subtract."}, {"id": "l3", "concept": "loolr", "level": "moderate", "type": "mcq", "stem": "In LOOCV with $n$ points you fit the line...", "options": [{"t": "$n$ times, once per left-out point", "ok": true, "why": "Each round leaves a different point out."}, {"t": "once on all points", "ok": false, "why": "That is training."}, {"t": "$n-1$ times", "ok": false, "why": "Once per point."}, {"t": "never", "ok": false, "why": "We refit."}], "explain": "$n$ refits.", "hint": "One per point."}]}, {"id": "comp", "title": "Model comparison: zero training error, geometry and true/false facts", "html": "\n<h3>Which models can reach zero training error?</h3>\n<p>Zero training error means every training point is predicted correctly (or exactly, for regression).</p>\n<table><tr><th>Model</th><th>Zero training error guaranteed?</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Look-up rule $h(x_i)=y_i$</td><td>Yes</td><td>it repeats the label</td></tr><tr><td>Decision tree (grown fully, distinct points)</td><td>Yes</td><td>keep splitting until leaves are pure</td></tr><tr><td>$1$-NN (each point its own neighbour)</td><td>Yes</td><td>nearest point is itself</td></tr><tr><td>$k$-NN with $k=5$</td><td>No</td><td>a point can be outvoted</td></tr><tr><td>Naive Bayes</td><td>No</td><td>its assumptions can force mistakes</td></tr><tr><td>Linear model $w^Tx+b$</td><td>No</td><td>a straight line cannot fit every pattern, even if $n>d+1$</td></tr><tr><td>Predict the mean $\\frac1n\\sum y_j$ or $0$</td><td>No</td><td>ignores the input</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Geometry of the decision or fit</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Model</th><th>Boundary shape</th></tr><tr><td>Decision tree</td><td>axis-aligned (splits $x_j\\le t$)</td></tr><tr><td>Ridge and Lasso regression</td><td>linear (a hyperplane)</td></tr><tr><td>$1$-NN</td><td>non-linear, piecewise</td></tr><tr><td>Gaussian Naive Bayes (different variances per class)</td><td>non-linear, curved (quadratic)</td></tr></table>\n<h3>True / false facts that keep appearing</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Statement</th><th>True?</th></tr><tr><td>Decision trees are a supervised method for classification</td><td>True</td></tr><tr><td>k-NN is lazy (no explicit model built at training)</td><td>True</td></tr><tr><td>In k-NN $k$ is the number of features</td><td>False ($k$ counts neighbours)</td></tr><tr><td>k-NN is parametric and needs fitted distributions</td><td>False</td></tr><tr><td>Depth of a tree is a hyperparameter, typically chosen by cross-validation</td><td>True</td></tr><tr><td>A question $f_k\\le\\theta$ always splits the data into two non-empty sets</td><td>False (if $\\theta$ is outside the data range one side is empty)</td></tr><tr><td>A \"better\" question has greater information gain</td><td>True (that is how \"better\" is defined)</td></tr><tr><td>Trees overfit if the maximum depth is too low</td><td>False (they underfit)</td></tr><tr><td>Trees underfit if the maximum depth is too low</td><td>True</td></tr><tr><td>The training set is not needed to predict with k-NN</td><td>False (it is needed)</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Trap: overfit vs underfit with depth</div><p>Too deep $\\Rightarrow$ overfit. Too shallow $\\Rightarrow$ underfit. A \"too low depth overfits\" option is always false.</p></div>\n<h3>Why each model can or cannot reach zero training error <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3>\n<table><tr><th>Model</th><th>Distinct points, any labels</th><th>Reason</th></tr><tr><td>fully grown tree</td><td>yes</td><td>keep splitting to pure leaves</td></tr><tr><td>$1$-NN (own neighbour)</td><td>yes</td><td>nearest point is itself</td></tr><tr><td>$k$-NN, $k\\ge2$</td><td>no</td><td>outvoted by neighbours</td></tr><tr><td>linear classifier</td><td>no</td><td>XOR-type labels cannot be split by a line</td></tr><tr><td>naive Bayes</td><td>no</td><td>independence can force mistakes</td></tr></table>\n<details class=\"rc\"><summary>Why can a linear classifier fail even with distinct points?</summary><p>Labels in an XOR pattern are not separable by a single line.</p></details>\n<div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example: zero training error or not</div><p>Four points $x=1,2,4,8$ with labels $0,1,0,1$. <b>1-NN:</b> every point is its own nearest neighbour, so training error is $0$. <b>3-NN</b> (each point counts itself): $x=2$ has neighbours $2,1,4$ with labels $1,0,0$, so it is predicted $0$ and is wrong; the other three are right, so training error is $1/4$. <b>One threshold</b> $x\\le\\theta$ cannot reproduce the pattern $0,1,0,1$, so it makes at least one training mistake.</p></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "m1", "concept": "zero", "level": "easy", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Which is certain to reach zero training error on distinct points?", "options": [{"t": "a fully grown decision tree", "ok": true, "why": "Leaves can be made pure."}, {"t": "$5$-NN", "ok": false, "why": "Voting can overrule a point."}, {"t": "a linear model", "ok": false, "why": "Not guaranteed."}, {"t": "predicting the mean", "ok": false, "why": "Ignores inputs."}], "explain": "Pure leaves.", "hint": "Can it keep splitting?"}, {"id": "m2", "concept": "compare", "level": "moderate", "type": "mcq", "stem": "The decision boundary of a decision tree is...", "options": [{"t": "axis-aligned", "ok": true, "why": "Splits are $x_j\\le t$."}, {"t": "always a circle", "ok": false, "why": "No."}, {"t": "always one line at 45 degrees", "ok": false, "why": "No."}, {"t": "curved quadratic", "ok": false, "why": "That is Gaussian NB."}], "explain": "Axis-aligned.", "hint": "Look at the form of a split."}, {"id": "m3", "concept": "compare", "level": "moderate", "type": "msq", "stem": "Select all true.", "options": [{"t": "k-NN is a lazy learner.", "ok": true, "why": "No training phase."}, {"t": "In k-NN $k$ is the number of features.", "ok": false, "why": "Neighbours."}, {"t": "Tree depth is chosen using cross-validation.", "ok": true, "why": "Standard practice."}, {"t": "Trees overfit when the maximum depth is too low.", "ok": false, "why": "They underfit."}], "explain": "Lazy, CV, underfit.", "hint": "Check each."}, {"id": "m4", "concept": "compare", "level": "hard", "type": "mcq", "stem": "\"A question $f_k\\le\\theta$ always partitions the data into two non-empty sets.\" This is...", "options": [{"t": "False", "ok": true, "why": "If $\\theta$ is beyond all values one side is empty."}, {"t": "True", "ok": false, "why": "Not guaranteed."}, {"t": "True only for numerical features", "ok": false, "why": "Same issue."}, {"t": "True if $\theta$ is an integer", "ok": false, "why": "Irrelevant."}], "explain": "Choose $\\theta$ outside the data.", "hint": "Try a threshold larger than every value."}]}, {"id": "pyqs", "title": "All PYQs of this topic, solved", "kind": "pyq", "html": "\n<p>All <b>13 PYQs</b> of the topic are below, in the bank's wording, with the exam label, question number and marks. <b>Attempt first.</b> Choose or type your answer, say how sure you are, press Submit. The full solution opens only after Submit or after \"I give up, show me\". Wrong answers are saved to the Mistake review page.</p>\n<h3>Conventions on this page</h3><ul><li>Tables from the bank are reproduced in the passage box.</li><li>Samples are numbered from $1$ in table order.</li><li>\"Better question\" is taken to mean higher information gain (see the flags).</li><li>Exam dates are shown as the bank gives them.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"notice\">Keys: <span class=\"tag SRC\">[SRC]</span> = key in the bank (4 questions); <span class=\"tag DERIVED\">[DERIVED]</span> = bank has no key, derived here (9 questions); <span class=\"tag FLAG\">[FLAG]</span> = my calculation and the bank disagree (0 questions: Mar 2024 Q13 is assumed to refer to the same left-out point (sample 3) as Q12. Mar 2024 Q20 and Dec 2024 Q11: the statement about choosing depth by cross-validation is taken as true.). Figures that the bank shows as images are described in words.</div>\n<h3>Pattern map <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3>\n<p>Built from the question IDs on this page; no question has been added.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>PYQs</th><th>First move</th></tr><tr><td>Choose a setting from a table</td><td>cv01, cv02</td><td>lowest validation error; check the gap</td></tr><tr><td>LOOCV by hand</td><td>cv03, cv04, cv05</td><td>drop the point, fit, predict</td></tr><tr><td>Count models and sizes</td><td>cv06, cv07</td><td>k models, n(k-1)/k</td></tr><tr><td>Zero training error</td><td>cv08, cv12</td><td>distinct points; own neighbour</td></tr><tr><td>True/false on models</td><td>cv09, cv10, cv11, cv13</td><td>geometry and definitions</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Variations to practise <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span> (not PYQs)</h3>\n<ul><li>$n=360$, $k=6$: all counts.</li><li>Three $\\lambda$ values with $k=5$: total models.</li><li>LOOCV on three points of your own.</li><li>Which model reaches zero training error when two points share the same $x$ but different labels?</li></ul>\n"}, {"id": "revision", "title": "Revision page", "html": "\n<h3>Definitions to know cold</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Validation set</td><td>held-out data used to pick hyperparameters</td></tr><tr><td>k-fold CV</td><td>split into $k$ folds, train on $k-1$, test on 1, repeat $k$ times</td></tr><tr><td>LOOCV</td><td>$k=n$: leave one point out each time</td></tr><tr><td>Overfitting</td><td>low training error, high validation error</td></tr><tr><td>Underfitting</td><td>both errors high</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Must-remember facts</h3>\n<ul><li>Choose the setting with the lowest <b>validation</b> error, not the lowest training error.</li><li>$k$-fold CV for one setting trains $k$ models, each on $n(k-1)/k$ points.</li><li>Several values of $\\lambda$: total models $=$ (number of values) $\\times k$.</li><li>LOOCV: fit on the other $n-1$ points, predict the left-out point.</li><li>Simple line: $w_1=S_{xy}/S_{xx}$, $w_0=\\bar y-w_1\\bar x$.</li><li>Guaranteed zero training error: look-up rule, full decision tree, $1$-NN. Not $k$-NN with $k>1$, not Naive Bayes, not a linear model in general.</li><li>Tree boundary axis-aligned; ridge and lasso linear; $1$-NN and Gaussian NB curved.</li><li>k-NN is lazy, non-parametric, needs the training data at prediction time; $k$ is neighbours.</li><li>Tree depth: hyperparameter chosen by CV; too low underfits.</li><li>$f_k\\le\\theta$ may leave one side empty.</li></ul>\n<h3>Recognition cues</h3>\n<table><tr><th>If the question says...</th><th>First move</th></tr><tr><td>table of training and validation errors</td><td>lowest validation error</td></tr><tr><td>\"k-fold ... how many models\"</td><td>$k$ (times number of settings)</td></tr><tr><td>\"training set size in k-fold\"</td><td>$n(k-1)/k$</td></tr><tr><td>\"left out\", \"validation point\"</td><td>fit without it, predict it</td></tr><tr><td>\"guaranteed zero training error\"</td><td>does it memorise labels?</td></tr><tr><td>\"matching models to geometry\"</td><td>tree axis, ridge/lasso linear, kNN/GNB curved</td></tr><tr><td>\"select all true\" about trees and kNN</td><td>use the facts table</td></tr></table>\n<h3>How to start each kind of question <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3>\n<figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 680 250\" style=\"width:680px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Flowchart for cross-validation questions\"><defs><marker id=\"cf\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" style=\"fill:var(--muted)\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"240\" y=\"8\" width=\"200\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.4\"/><text x=\"340.0\" y=\"30.0\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-weight:700;\">Read the question</text><text x=\"340.0\" y=\"49.0\" font-size=\"10.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">what is asked?</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"100\" width=\"150\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--ex-bg);stroke:var(--ex-bd);stroke-width:1.4\"/><text x=\"85.0\" y=\"122.0\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-weight:700;\">Pick a setting</text><text x=\"85.0\" y=\"141.0\" font-size=\"10.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">lowest validation error</text><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"98\" marker-end=\"url(#cf)\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);stroke-width:1.6;\"/><rect x=\"180\" y=\"100\" width=\"150\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--ex-bg);stroke:var(--ex-bd);stroke-width:1.4\"/><text x=\"255.0\" y=\"122.0\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-weight:700;\">Count models</text><text x=\"255.0\" y=\"141.0\" font-size=\"10.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">k per setting x settings</text><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"255\" y2=\"98\" marker-end=\"url(#cf)\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);stroke-width:1.6;\"/><rect x=\"350\" y=\"100\" width=\"150\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--ex-bg);stroke:var(--ex-bd);stroke-width:1.4\"/><text x=\"425.0\" y=\"122.0\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-weight:700;\">LOOCV by hand</text><text x=\"425.0\" y=\"141.0\" font-size=\"10.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">refit without that point</text><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"425\" y2=\"98\" marker-end=\"url(#cf)\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);stroke-width:1.6;\"/><rect x=\"520\" y=\"100\" width=\"150\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--bad-bg);stroke:var(--bad-bd);stroke-width:1.4\"/><text x=\"595.0\" y=\"122.0\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-weight:700;\">True / false</text><text x=\"595.0\" y=\"141.0\" font-size=\"10.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--muted);\">check zero-error rule</text><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"595\" y2=\"98\" marker-end=\"url(#cf)\" style=\"stroke:var(--muted);stroke-width:1.6;\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"184\" width=\"660\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" style=\"fill:var(--def-bg);stroke:var(--def-bd);stroke-width:1.4\"/><text x=\"340.0\" y=\"206.0\" font-size=\"11.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);font-weight:700;\">Checks: 1. training size is n(k-1)/k, not n/k.  2. Leave the point OUT before fitting.</text><text x=\"340.0\" y=\"226.0\" font-size=\"11.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"fill:var(--ink);\">3. Several settings multiply the count.  4. Distinct points and 1-NN: zero training error.</text></svg><figcaption>Find the case, then follow the checks.</figcaption></figure>\n<h3>Recall: six quick questions <span class=\"tag ADDED\">[ADDED]</span></h3>\n<details class=\"rc\"><summary>Which error picks the setting?</summary><p>Validation error.</p></details><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Models in $k$-fold for one setting?</summary><p>$k$.</p></details><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Training size in $k$-fold?</summary><p>$n(k-1)/k$.</p></details><details class=\"rc\"><summary>LOOCV training size?</summary><p>$n-1$.</p></details><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Is $k$-NN lazy?</summary><p>Yes.</p></details><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Why keep a test set?</summary><p>To report an honest final score after all choices are made.</p></details>\n"}, {"id": "test", "title": "Topic test: mixed questions", "kind": "test", "html": "<p>Eight mixed questions. Give an honest confidence for each; the summary ranks weak concepts.</p>"}, {"id": "checklist", "title": "Checklist: skills you should be able to do", "kind": "checklist", "html": "<p>Tick a skill only after doing it without notes.</p>"}], "pyqs": [{"id": "cv01", "exam": "Aug 2025 (03 Aug)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q4", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "tune", "tag": "DERIVED", "context": "<table><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Learning rate</th><th>Iterations</th><th>Training error</th><th>Validation error</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0.05</td><td>1000</td><td>150</td><td>160</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.10</td><td>800</td><td>145</td><td>150</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.15</td><td>500</td><td>140</td><td>190</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.25</td><td>350</td><td>170</td><td>180</td></tr></table>", "wording": "You are evaluating the performance of a linear regression model using different hyperparameter settings (learning rate and number of iterations). Below are the results. Which hyperparameter setting would you choose based on the training and validation errors? Why?", "options": ["Setting 3, because it has the lowest training error, indicating the best fit to the training data.", "Setting 2, because it has the lowest validation error and a relatively low training error, indicating a good generalization.", "Setting 1, because it has the highest number of iterations and therefore likely to be more stable.", "Setting 4, because it uses the highest learning rate and converges the fastest."], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "tagNote": "The bank gives no key. Derived by working the question.", "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the best setting and the reason.</p>", "idea": "<p>Choose the setting with the lowest validation error; check that the gap to the training error is small.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Validation errors are $160,\\ 150,\\ 190,\\ 180$ for settings $1,2,3,4$. The lowest is $150$ (setting 2).</li><li>Setting 3 has the lowest training error ($140$) but a validation error of $190$: gap $50$, which signals overfitting.</li><li>Setting 4 has both errors high ($170$, $180$), which signals underfitting.</li><li>Setting 2 has validation $150$ and training $145$: gap only $5$, good generalisation.</li></ol>", "optWhy": ["Wrong: the lowest training error is not the criterion, and the large gap shows overfitting.", "<b>Correct.</b> Lowest validation error with a small gap.", "Wrong: more iterations are not evidence of quality; setting 1's validation error ($160$) is higher than setting 2's.", "Wrong: a high learning rate is not a reason, and its errors are high."], "others": "<p>Gaps (validation minus training): setting 1: $10$; setting 2: $5$; setting 3: $50$; setting 4: $10$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: the setting with the lowest validation error is $2$ ✓.</p>", "trap": "Picking the lowest training error.", "rule": "<p>Pick the lowest validation error.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "If setting 3's validation error were $140$ instead of $190$, which would win?", "a": "Setting 3 (validation $140$ would be the lowest)."}}, "sub": "Cross-Validation & Hyperparameter Tuning"}, {"id": "cv02", "exam": "Apr 2026 (06 Apr)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q2", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "tune", "tag": "DERIVED", "context": "<table><tr><th>$\\lambda$</th><th>Avg. training error (MSE)</th><th>Avg. validation error (MSE)</th></tr><tr><td>0.001</td><td>1.25</td><td>8.4</td></tr><tr><td>0.1</td><td>2.1</td><td>4.2</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>3.45</td><td>3.15</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>5.8</td><td>3.2</td></tr><tr><td>100</td><td>12.4</td><td>11.9</td></tr></table>", "wording": "In Ridge Regression, the optimal weight vector is given by $\\hat w=(XX^T+\\lambda I)^{-1}Xy$. The regularization parameter $\\lambda$ is determined using 5-fold cross-validation. The table summarizes the average training and validation errors (MSE) for various values of $\\lambda$. Based on the cross-validation results provided, what is the most appropriate choice for $\\lambda$?", "options": ["$0.001$", "$0.1$", "$1.0$", "$10.0$", "$100$"], "key": {"opts": [2]}, "tagNote": "The bank gives no key. Derived by working the question.", "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the best $\\lambda$ from the table.</p>", "idea": "<p>The lowest average validation error.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Validation errors: $8.40,\\ 4.20,\\ 3.15,\\ 3.20,\\ 11.90$.</li><li>The smallest is $3.15$, at $\\lambda=1.0$.</li><li>Check the pattern: for $\\lambda=0.001$ the training error ($1.25$) is far below validation ($8.40$): overfitting. For $\\lambda=100$ both are large: underfitting.</li></ol>", "optWhy": ["Wrong: tiny training error but a huge validation error (overfit).", "Wrong: validation error $4.20$ is higher than $3.15$.", "<b>Correct.</b> Lowest validation error $3.15$.", "Wrong: $3.20$ is close but not the lowest.", "Wrong: both errors are large (underfit)."], "others": "<p>With $\\lambda=10$ the error is only $0.05$ higher; if the question asked for a simpler model within noise one might prefer it, but \"most appropriate\" by validation error is $1.0$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: best $\\lambda=1$ ✓.</p>", "trap": "Picking $\\lambda=0.001$ for its low training error.", "rule": "<p>Pick the $\\lambda$ with the lowest validation error.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "If $\\lambda=10$ had validation $3.10$?", "a": "Then $\\lambda=10$ would be the choice."}}, "sub": "Cross-Validation & Hyperparameter Tuning"}, {"id": "cv03", "exam": "Dec 2024 (01 Dec)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q8", "marks": 4, "type": "SA", "concept": "loolr", "tag": "SRC", "wording": "Consider the following dataset: $D=\\{(1,2),(2,2),(0,1)\\}$. Assume that Leave one out cross validation is applied on this dataset and the model used is $y=w_0+w_1x$. Suppose $w=[w_0,w_1]^T$ be the weight obtained when $(2,2)$ is used in the validation set. Then calculate $\\|w\\|_2^2$.", "key": {"lo": 2, "hi": 2, "show": "2"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\|w\\|_2^2$ for the line fitted when $(2,2)$ is left out.</p>", "idea": "<p>Fit on the other two points, which define the line exactly.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Training points: $(1,2)$ and $(0,1)$.</li><li>Slope $w_1=(2-1)/(1-0)=1$.</li><li>Intercept: at $x=0$ the line gives $y=1$, so $w_0=1$.</li><li>$\\|w\\|_2^2=w_0^2+w_1^2=1+1=2$.</li></ol>", "range": "<p>Exactly $2$ ✓.</p>", "others": "<p>The validation point $(2,2)$ is not used for fitting. (The line predicts $3$ there, error $1$.)</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $w=(1,1)$, norm$^2$ $2$ ✓.</p>", "trap": "Fitting on all three points.", "rule": "<p>Fit on the points that are not left out.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Left out $(1,2)$ instead: $\\|w\\|^2$?", "a": "Train on $(2,2),(0,1)$: slope $0.5$, intercept $1$; $\\|w\\|^2=1.25$."}}, "sub": "Cross-Validation & Hyperparameter Tuning"}, {"id": "cv04", "exam": "Mar 2024 (24 Mar)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q13", "marks": 4, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "loolr", "tag": "DERIVED", "context": "Dataset (sample number, $X$, $y$):<table><tr><th>Sample No</th><th>$X$</th><th>$y$</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>5</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>6</td><td>13</td></tr></table>Linear regression model $y=w_0+w_1x$, with Leave-One-Out Cross-Validation. The question refers to the same left-out point as the neighbouring part, where the $3^{\\text{rd}}$ sample is the validation point.", "wording": "What will be the predicted value for the left-out data point?", "options": ["$12$", "$13$", "$12.3$", "$11.3$", "None of these"], "key": {"opts": [3]}, "tagNote": "The bank gives no key. It is assumed that the left-out point is the 3rd sample (as in the neighbouring Q12); the result 11.33 then matches option 4.", "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the prediction for the left-out point (sample 3, $x=5$) from the line fitted on the other three samples.</p>", "idea": "<p>Fit the line on $(3,8),(0,3),(6,13)$, then evaluate at $x=5$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$\\bar x=3$, $\\bar y=8$.</li><li>$S_{xx}=0+9+9=18$; $S_{xy}=0+15+15=30$.</li><li>$w_1=30/18=1.6667$; $w_0=8-1.6667\\cdot3=3.0000$.</li><li>Prediction at $x=5$: $3+1.6667\\cdot5=11.333$. To one decimal place: $11.3$.</li></ol>", "optWhy": ["Wrong: $12$ is the true label, not the prediction.", "Wrong: $13$ belongs to another sample.", "Wrong: $12.3$ would come from a different fit.", "<b>Correct.</b> $11.33\\approx11.3$.", "Wrong: option 4 matches."], "others": "<p>The error for this point is $12-11.33=0.67$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $w_0=3.000$, $w_1=1.667$, prediction 11.333$ ✓.</p>", "trap": "Reporting the true label $12$.", "rule": "<p>Predict the left-out point with a model that did not see it.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Prediction at $x=0$ from the same line?", "a": "$w_0=3$."}}, "sub": "Cross-Validation & Hyperparameter Tuning"}, {"id": "cv05", "exam": "Mar 2024 (24 Mar)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q12", "marks": 4, "type": "SA", "concept": "loolr", "tag": "SRC", "context": "Dataset (sample number, $X$, $y$):<table><tr><th>Sample No</th><th>$X$</th><th>$y$</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>5</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>6</td><td>13</td></tr></table>Linear regression model $y=w_0+w_1x$, with Leave-One-Out Cross-Validation.", "wording": "Enter the value of $w_1$ obtained when the $3^{\\text{rd}}$ sample is used as the validation data point.", "key": {"lo": 1.65, "hi": 1.7, "show": "1.65 to 1.70"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the slope $w_1$ when sample 3 $(5,12)$ is left out.</p>", "idea": "<p>Fit the line on samples 1, 2 and 4.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Training points: $(3,8),(0,3),(6,13)$.</li><li>$\\bar x=(3+0+6)/3=3$, $\\bar y=(8+3+13)/3=8$.</li><li>$S_{xx}=(3-3)^2+(0-3)^2+(6-3)^2=18$.</li><li>$S_{xy}=(3-3)(8-8)+(0-3)(3-8)+(6-3)(13-8)=0+15+15=30$.</li><li>$w_1=30/18=1.6667$.</li></ol>", "range": "<p>Accepted $1.65$ to $1.70$; $1.667$ ✓.</p>", "others": "<p>$w_0=8-1.667\\cdot3=3$, giving prediction $11.33$ at $x=5$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: 1.6667 ✓.</p>", "trap": "Including the left-out point in the fit.", "rule": "<p>$w_1=S_{xy}/S_{xx}$ on the training points only.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$w_1$ if sample 2 $(0,3)$ is left out?", "a": "1.714 (fit on $(3,8),(5,12),(6,13)$)."}}, "sub": "Cross-Validation & Hyperparameter Tuning"}, {"id": "cv06", "exam": "Aug 2024 (04 Aug)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q10", "marks": 1.5, "type": "SA", "concept": "cvproc", "tag": "SRC", "context": "A dataset for ridge regression has $1000$ data-points. $k$-fold cross validation is performed to estimate the value of $\\lambda$ in ridge regression with $k=4$. For a given value of $\\lambda$:", "wording": "How many models have to be trained?", "key": {"lo": 4, "hi": 4, "show": "4"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> models trained for one $\\lambda$ in $4$-fold CV.</p>", "idea": "<p>One model per fold.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>There are $k=4$ folds.</li><li>Each fold takes a turn as validation data, and a model is trained on the other three.</li><li>That is $4$ models.</li></ol>", "range": "<p>Exactly $4$ ✓.</p>", "others": "<p>With $5$ candidate values of $\\lambda$ we would train $5\\times4=20$ models.</p>", "verify": "<p>$k=4$ ✓.</p>", "trap": "Answering $1000$ or $250$.", "rule": "<p>Models for one setting $=k$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$10$-fold CV for one $\\lambda$?", "a": "$10$ models."}}, "sub": "Cross-Validation & Hyperparameter Tuning"}, {"id": "cv07", "exam": "Aug 2024 (04 Aug)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q11", "marks": 1.5, "type": "SA", "concept": "cvproc", "tag": "SRC", "context": "A dataset for ridge regression has $1000$ data-points. $4$-fold cross validation is performed to estimate $\\lambda$.", "wording": "While training each of these models, how many data-points does the training set have?", "key": {"lo": 750, "hi": 750, "show": "750"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the size of each training set.</p>", "idea": "<p>Remove one fold of $1000/4$ points.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Each fold has $1000/4=250$ points.</li><li>The training set is the other $3$ folds: $3\\times250=750$.</li><li>Equivalent: $1000\\cdot\\frac{k-1}{k}=1000\\cdot\\frac34=750$.</li></ol>", "range": "<p>Exactly $750$ ✓.</p>", "others": "<p>The validation set of each round has $250$ points.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: 750 ✓.</p>", "trap": "Answering $250$ (the validation size).", "rule": "<p>Training size $=n(k-1)/k$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$n=900$, $k=3$?", "a": "$600$."}}, "sub": "Cross-Validation & Hyperparameter Tuning"}, {"id": "cv08", "exam": "Apr 2026 (06 Apr)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q3", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "zero", "tag": "DERIVED", "wording": "Consider a training dataset $(x_i,y_i)_{i=1}^n$ where all input points $x_i\\in\\mathbb R^d$ are distinct. Which of the following models is guaranteed to achieve a training error of zero?", "options": ["$h(x_i)=\\frac1n\\sum_{j=1}^ny_j\\quad\\forall i$", "$h(x_i)=0\\quad\\forall i$", "$h(x_i)=w^Tx_i+b\\quad\\forall i$ given $n>d+1$", "$h(x_i)=y_i\\quad\\forall i$"], "key": {"opts": [3]}, "tagNote": "The bank gives no key. Derived by working the question.", "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the model that is guaranteed to have zero training error.</p>", "idea": "<p>Zero error means $h(x_i)=y_i$ for every training point.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1 predicts the mean for every point; correct only if all labels are equal. Not guaranteed.</li><li>Option 2 predicts $0$ everywhere; correct only if all labels are $0$. Not guaranteed.</li><li>Option 3 is a straight line/hyperplane with $d+1$ parameters; with $n>d+1$ points there are more equations than unknowns, so exact fit is generally impossible. Not guaranteed.</li><li>Option 4 sets $h(x_i)=y_i$ by definition. Guaranteed.</li></ol>", "optWhy": ["Wrong: constant prediction.", "Wrong: constant zero.", "Wrong: too few parameters for $n>d+1$ points in general.", "<b>Correct.</b> It returns each training label."], "others": "<p>Option 3 with $n\\le d+1$ distinct generic points could fit exactly, but the condition given is $n>d+1$, which removes that guarantee.</p>", "verify": "<p>Counter-example for option 3: points $(0,0),(1,1),(2,0)$ with $d=1$, $n=3>2$: no line passes through all three ✓.</p>", "trap": "Choosing option 3 because \"linear models fit well\".", "rule": "<p>Only a model that can memorise the labels is guaranteed zero training error.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Is a fully grown tree guaranteed zero error on distinct points?", "a": "Yes: it can isolate every point."}}, "sub": "Model Comparison & General Supervised Learning Properties"}, {"id": "cv09", "exam": "Apr 2026 (06 Apr)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q4", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "compare", "tag": "DERIVED", "context": "Models: 1. Decision Tree; 2. Gaussian Naive Bayes; 3. Ridge Regression; 4. 1-Nearest Neighbor; 5. Lasso Regression. Geometry: (A) Axis-Aligned; (B) Linear Hyperplane; (C) Non-Linear / Curved.", "wording": "Consider the following classification models and their geometric properties. Select the correct many-to-one matching:", "options": ["$\\{1\\to A\\};\\{2,3,4\\to B\\};\\{5\\to C\\}$", "$\\{1\\to A\\};\\{3,5\\to B\\};\\{2,4\\to C\\}$", "$\\{1,3\\to A\\};\\{2,5\\to B\\};\\{4\\to C\\}$", "$\\{1\\to A\\};\\{2,4\\to B\\};\\{3,5\\to C\\}$"], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "tagNote": "The bank gives no key. Derived by working the question.", "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the correct pairing of models with geometry.</p>", "idea": "<p>Trees cut along axes; ridge and lasso are linear; Gaussian Naive Bayes (class-specific variances) and $1$-NN give curved/non-linear boundaries.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Decision Tree $\\to$ (A) axis-aligned.</li><li>Ridge and Lasso $\\to$ (B) linear hyperplane.</li><li>Gaussian Naive Bayes $\\to$ (C) curved (log-ratio of Gaussians with different variances is quadratic).</li><li>$1$-NN $\\to$ (C) non-linear piecewise.</li><li>So $\\{1\\to A\\};\\{3,5\\to B\\};\\{2,4\\to C\\}$.</li></ol>", "optWhy": ["Wrong: puts Gaussian NB and $1$-NN in the linear group and lasso as curved.", "<b>Correct.</b>", "Wrong: ridge regression is linear, not axis-aligned.", "Wrong: $1$-NN and Gaussian NB are not linear, and ridge/lasso are not curved."], "others": "<p>The Naive Bayes boundary becomes linear only when all class variances are equal; in general it is curved.</p>", "verify": "<p>Each model appears exactly once ✓.</p>", "trap": "Putting Gaussian NB with linear models because it is \"simple\".", "rule": "<p>Tree: axis-aligned. Ridge/Lasso: linear. k-NN and Gaussian NB: curved.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Which model here gives rectangular regions?", "a": "The decision tree."}}, "sub": "Model Comparison & General Supervised Learning Properties"}, {"id": "cv10", "exam": "Dec 2023 (day not given)", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q8", "marks": 4, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "compare", "tag": "DERIVED", "wording": "Select all the statements that are true about decision trees and $k$-Nearest Neighbors ($k$-NN) in machine learning:", "options": ["Decision trees are a supervised learning algorithm used for classifications.", "The $k$-NN algorithm is a lazy learner, which means it doesn't build an explicit model during the training phase.", "In $k$-NN, the value of $k$ represents the number of features used for classification.", "$k$-Nearest Neighbors ($k$-NN) is a parametric model that requires estimating probability distributions.", "The depth of the tree is a hyperparameter and is typically chosen using cross-validation."], "key": {"opts": [0, 1, 4]}, "tagNote": "The bank gives no key. Derived by working the question.", "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the true statements.</p>", "idea": "<p>Check each against the standard facts.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Trees are supervised and classify. True.</li><li>k-NN stores the data and does the work at prediction time: lazy. True.</li><li>$k$ is the number of neighbours, not features. False.</li><li>k-NN is non-parametric and does not estimate distributions. False.</li><li>Depth is a hyperparameter selected by cross-validation. True.</li></ol>", "optWhy": ["<b>True.</b>", "<b>True.</b>", "False.", "False.", "<b>True.</b>"], "others": "<p>\"Lazy\" means no training work; the cost moves to prediction.</p>", "verify": "<p>Matches the revision facts ✓.</p>", "trap": "Ticking option 3 or 4.", "rule": "<p>k-NN: lazy, non-parametric, $k$ = neighbours.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Is naive Bayes lazy?", "a": "No: it estimates parameters at training time."}}, "sub": "Model Comparison & General Supervised Learning Properties"}, {"id": "cv11", "exam": "Mar 2024 (24 Mar)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q20", "marks": 4, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "compare", "tag": "DERIVED", "wording": "Select all true statements.", "options": ["In Decision tree, if a question $Q_1$ is \"better\" than question $Q_2$, then information gains for $Q_1$ is greater than information gains $Q_2$ always.", "The training dataset is required while predicting the label of a test-point in the $k$-NN algorithm.", "A question of the form $f_k\\le\\theta$ always partitions the dataset into two non-empty sets.", "The depth of the tree is a hyperparameter and has to be chosen using cross validation.", "Decision trees are prone to overfit if the maximum depth is set too low."], "key": {"opts": [0, 1, 3]}, "tagNote": "The bank gives no key. Option 1 is true because \"better\" is defined by higher information gain. Option 4 is judged true because cross-validation is the standard way to choose depth (a plain validation set would also be acceptable in practice, so the word \"has to\" is slightly strong).", "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the true statements.</p>", "idea": "<p>Judge each statement against the definition of the tree criterion and the k-NN facts.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>\"Better\" question means higher information gain (lower weighted impurity). So the better question has the larger gain. True.</li><li>k-NN needs the stored training set at prediction time. True.</li><li>If $\\theta$ is above every value of $f_k$, all points go to the \"yes\" side and the other side is empty. False.</li><li>Depth is a hyperparameter chosen by cross-validation. True.</li><li>Too low depth causes underfitting, not overfitting. False.</li></ol>", "optWhy": ["<b>True.</b> That is how \"better\" is defined.", "<b>True.</b>", "False: a threshold outside the data range gives an empty side.", "<b>True</b> (standard practice).", "False: a shallow tree underfits."], "others": "<p>Compare with the Dec 2024 version where \"the training dataset is <b>not</b> required\" is false.</p>", "verify": "<p>Counter-example for option 3: all values of $f_k$ are $\\le5$ and $\\theta=10$: every point satisfies the question ✓.</p>", "trap": "Ticking option 5.", "rule": "<p>Shallow tree: underfit. Deep tree: overfit.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Which depth risks overfitting?", "a": "A very large depth."}}, "sub": "Model Comparison & General Supervised Learning Properties"}, {"id": "cv12", "exam": "Aug 2024 (04 Aug)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q2", "marks": 3, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "zero", "tag": "DERIVED", "wording": "Which of the following classifiers are certainly capable of achieving zero training error for any binary classification problem with features in $\\mathbb R^2$? Note: while computing the training error for KNN, each data-point is its own neighbor.", "options": ["Decision tree", "KNN with $k=1$", "KNN with $k=5$", "Naive Bayes"], "key": {"opts": [0, 1]}, "tagNote": "The bank gives no key. Derived by working the question.", "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> classifiers that always reach zero training error.</p>", "idea": "<p>A model reaches zero error always only if it can memorise the training labels.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Decision tree: keep splitting until every leaf is pure. Yes (for distinct points).</li><li>$1$-NN: each point's nearest neighbour is itself. Yes.</li><li>$5$-NN: a point can be outvoted by four neighbours of the other class. No.</li><li>Naive Bayes: its independence assumption and simple likelihoods can force mistakes on some data sets. No.</li></ol>", "optWhy": ["<b>Certain.</b>", "<b>Certain.</b>", "Not certain.", "Not certain."], "others": "<p>Example for $k=5$: a lone $+$ point inside a cloud of $-$ points is outvoted by its four nearest $-$ neighbours.</p>", "verify": "<p>Consistent with the list in the model comparison section ✓.</p>", "trap": "Ticking $5$-NN because \"k-NN overfits\".", "rule": "<p>Memorising models (tree, $1$-NN) reach zero training error.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Does $3$-NN always reach zero?", "a": "No (a point can be outvoted $2$ to $1$)."}}, "sub": "Model Comparison & General Supervised Learning Properties"}, {"id": "cv13", "exam": "Dec 2024 (01 Dec)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q11", "marks": 4, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "compare", "tag": "DERIVED", "wording": "Select all true statements.", "options": ["In Decision tree, if a question $Q_1$ is \"better\" than question $Q_2$ then information gains for $Q_1$ is greater than information gains $Q_2$ always.", "The training dataset is not required while predicting the label of a test-point in the $k$-NN algorithm.", "A question of the form $f_k\\le\\theta$ always partitions the dataset into two non-empty sets.", "The depth of the tree is a hyperparameter and has to be chosen using cross validation.", "Decision trees are prone to underfit if the maximum depth is set too low."], "key": {"opts": [0, 3, 4]}, "tagNote": "The bank gives no key. Options 1 and 4 are judged true (see the March 2024 version: \"better\" means higher information gain; cross-validation is the standard way to choose depth).", "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the true statements.</p>", "idea": "<p>Same facts as the March 2024 version, with two statements changed.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>\"Better\" means higher information gain. True.</li><li>k-NN needs the training data at prediction time, so \"not required\" is False.</li><li>A threshold outside the data range leaves one side empty. False.</li><li>Depth is chosen by cross-validation. True.</li><li>A tree of too low depth underfits. True.</li></ol>", "optWhy": ["<b>True.</b>", "False: it is needed.", "False.", "<b>True.</b>", "<b>True.</b>"], "others": "<p>Differences from March 2024: option 2 is negated (now false) and option 5 says \"underfit\" (now true).</p>", "verify": "<p>Each option judged ✓.</p>", "trap": "Ticking option 2.", "rule": "<p>Read negations and the words \"overfit\" and \"underfit\" carefully.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "If option 2 said \"is required\"?", "a": "True."}}, "sub": "Model Comparison & General Supervised Learning Properties"}], "test": [{"id": "t1", "concept": "cvproc", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "$n=800$, $k=5$ folds. Training size per model?", "num": {"ans": 640, "tol": 0.001, "show": "640"}, "explain": "$800\\cdot4/5$.", "hint": "k-1 folds."}, {"id": "t2", "concept": "cvproc", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "$5$-fold CV over $3$ values of $\\lambda$: total models?", "num": {"ans": 15, "tol": 0.001, "show": "15"}, "explain": "$3\\times5$.", "hint": "Multiply."}, {"id": "t3", "concept": "tune", "level": "moderate", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Validation errors for $\\lambda=0.1,1,10$ are $5,4,6$. Which $\\lambda$?", "options": [{"t": "$1$", "ok": true, "why": "Lowest validation."}, {"t": "$0.1$", "ok": false, "why": "Higher."}, {"t": "$10$", "ok": false, "why": "Highest."}, {"t": "cannot say", "ok": false, "why": "Compare."}], "explain": "Lowest validation.", "hint": "Compare 5, 4, 6."}, {"id": "t4", "concept": "loolr", "level": "moderate", "type": "num", "stem": "Train points $(0,1),(2,5)$. Prediction at $x=1$?", "num": {"ans": 3, "tol": 0.001, "show": "3"}, "explain": "Slope $2$, intercept $1$: $3$.", "hint": "Fit exactly."}, {"id": "t5", "concept": "zero", "level": "moderate", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Which is guaranteed to reach zero training error on distinct points?", "options": [{"t": "$1$-NN", "ok": true, "why": "Nearest is itself."}, {"t": "$5$-NN", "ok": false, "why": "Outvoting."}, {"t": "linear classifier", "ok": false, "why": "Not always."}, {"t": "Naive Bayes", "ok": false, "why": "Not always."}], "explain": "Memorisers.", "hint": "Own neighbour."}, {"id": "t6", "concept": "compare", "level": "moderate", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Match: Ridge regression boundary is...", "options": [{"t": "linear", "ok": true, "why": "A hyperplane."}, {"t": "axis-aligned", "ok": false, "why": "Tree."}, {"t": "curved", "ok": false, "why": "k-NN."}, {"t": "circular", "ok": false, "why": "No."}], "explain": "Linear.", "hint": "Ridge is a linear model."}, {"id": "t7", "concept": "compare", "level": "moderate", "type": "msq", "stem": "Select all true.", "options": [{"t": "Tree depth is chosen by cross-validation.", "ok": true, "why": "Hyperparameter."}, {"t": "A tree with too low depth overfits.", "ok": false, "why": "Underfits."}, {"t": "k-NN needs the training data to predict.", "ok": true, "why": "Lazy."}, {"t": "$k$ in k-NN is the number of features.", "ok": false, "why": "Neighbours."}], "explain": "CV, data needed.", "hint": "Check each."}, {"id": "t8", "concept": "tune", "level": "hard", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Training error $1$, validation error $10$. Diagnosis?", "options": [{"t": "overfitting", "ok": true, "why": "Large gap."}, {"t": "underfitting", "ok": false, "why": "Needs both high."}, {"t": "good fit", "ok": false, "why": "Gap large."}, {"t": "no information", "ok": false, "why": "The gap tells us."}], "explain": "Gap.", "hint": "Compare the two."}], "checklist": [{"id": "c1", "t": "I can fit a line $y=w_0+w_1x$ to two or three points."}, {"id": "c2", "t": "I can read an error table and choose the best hyperparameter."}, {"id": "c3", "t": "I can diagnose overfitting and underfitting from training and validation errors."}, {"id": "c4", "t": "I can count the models and the training size in $k$-fold CV."}, {"id": "c5", "t": "I can run LOOCV for a line."}, {"id": "c6", "t": "I know which models are guaranteed zero training error."}, {"id": "c7", "t": "I can match models to boundary geometry."}, {"id": "c8", "t": "I can judge the true/false statements about trees and k-NN."}, {"id": "c9", "t": "I have attempted every PYQ and understood any I missed."}], "formulas": [{"name": "Simple line", "tex": "w_1=\\frac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}},\\ w_0=\\bar y-w_1\\bar x", "note": "S are sums of centred products."}, {"name": "k-fold counts", "tex": "\\text{models}=k,\\ \\ \\text{train size}=n\\frac{k-1}{k},\\ \\ \\text{validation size}=\\frac nk", "note": "Per setting."}, {"name": "LOOCV", "tex": "k=n:\\ n\\ \\text{models, each on } n-1\\ \\text{points}", "note": "Leave one out."}, {"name": "Validation rule", "tex": "\\lambda^*=\\arg\\min_\\lambda\\ \\text{validation error}", "note": "Not training error."}, {"name": "Total models in a grid search [ADDED]", "tex": "\\text{models}=(\\text{number of settings})\\times k", "note": "k-fold; leave-one-out has k = n."}]};